<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paleolithic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Old Stone Age, Palaeolithic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Paleolithic, or Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, is the period of human prehistory marked by the first development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, extending from their earliest known use by hominins about 3.3 million years ago to the end of the Pleistocene about 11,650 years before the present. People lived in small bands and subsisted by gathering plants, fishing, and hunting or scavenging wild animals, using knapped stone tools. From about 50,000 years ago artifacts became more varied and the first art appeared, and humans reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and finally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The term was coined by John Lubbock in 1865.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paleolithic.jpg" alt="Paleolithic" width="330" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nucleus_Brassempouy_Global_fond.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term Palaeolithic was coined by the archaeologist John Lubbock in 1865, from the Greek palaios, old, and lithos, stone. The period overlaps with the Pleistocene, during which the climate swung between glacial and interglacial phases. At its start hominins lived chiefly in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">Great Rift Valley</a>. Between about 2,000,000 and 1,500,000 years ago groups began leaving Africa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a> was occupied by about 1,700,000 years ago and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by about 1,660,000, and by the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-paleolithic">Lower Paleolithic</a> hominins lived in China, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as far north as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tool</a> industry, the Oldowan, produced choppers, burins and stitching awls. It was replaced by the more complex Acheulean industry, first conceived by Homo ergaster about 1.8 to 1.65 million years ago, and that by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-paleolithic">Middle Paleolithic</a> kits such as the Mousterian. By about 200,000 years ago the prepared-core technique allowed stone-tipped spears, the earliest composite tools. Harpoons appeared about 90,000 years ago, bringing fish into the diet.</p><p>About 50,000 years ago the diversity of artifacts increased markedly, and bone artifacts and the first art appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Humans reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> between about 50,000 and 40,000 years ago, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by about 30,000, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> above the Arctic Circle by about 27,000. By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-paleolithic">Upper Paleolithic</a> they had crossed Beringia and spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most knowledge of Paleolithic people comes from archaeology and comparison with modern hunter-gatherers such as the !Kung San. People lived in small bands, gathering plants, fishing, and hunting or scavenging animals, without states or organised governments. Population density was very low, around 0.4 inhabitants per square kilometre, since food limited the size of groups.</p><p>Art becomes common only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-paleolithic">Upper Paleolithic</a>, in cave paintings, rock art and jewellery, though earlier examples, such as ochre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> at Blombos Cave, come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-paleolithic">Middle Paleolithic</a>. Burials at Krapina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, about 130,000 years ago, and at Qafzeh in Israel, about 100,000 years ago, have led some scholars to infer a belief in an afterlife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paleolithic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The prehistory of Nubia goes back to the <b>Paleolithic</b>, around 300,000 years ago.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The island had been settled since the <b>Paleolithic</b> and Neolithic, and the cultures that followed, among them the Ozieri culture (3200–2700 BCE), left menhirs, dolmens, more than 2,400 rock-cut tombs called domus de Janas and the stepped platform of Monte d’Accoddi.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
